WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
AI-induced labor market shifts and aging workforce dynamics: a cross-national study of corporate responses in Japan, the USA and India.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.620
A technology career has always started at the bottom rung: basic coding, testing and data work. Experience gained there is how people move up.

3
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:11.220
AI now does much of that entry-level work. With fewer entry points, fewer newcomers ever reach the experience that senior roles demand.

4
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.220
At the top, senior staff consolidate their positions and stay longer, and pressure builds to raise the retirement age. Knowledge stays locked in a fixed group.

5
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.820
The result is a loop: each step makes it harder for the next generation to get in.

6
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:26.220
The study compares how fifteen leading companies respond, five in each of three countries at different stages of AI adoption and population aging.

7
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:32.220
The pressures differ by country. The U.S. leads in AI hiring and has moved pensions into 401(k) plans; over 28% of Japan's population is 65 or older; India's young workforce is shifting to the National Pension Scheme.

8
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.220
Comparing the case studies response by response: all three reskill workers, offer flexible work, move to defined-contribution pensions and fund financial literacy.

9
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.820
They differ where their demographics differ: only Japan's firms advocate a higher retirement age and use AI to support older workers; India prepares early with phased retirement.

10
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:46.120
Common responses, different contexts. Keeping experience and making room for new talent have to be balanced together.
